WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:08.559
LOCOMOTION is a boon to animals.

00:00:08.559 --> 00:00:13.510
We find plants moving, and animals
moving as well as locomoting.

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:16.410
So what’s the basic difference between the
two?

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:21.780
Well, movement is basically just moving a
particular part, while locomotion is

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:25.100
trans-locating from one place to another.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:30.119
Hence we say that plants only move,
while animals can carry out both, movement

00:00:30.119 --> 00:00:31.970
as well as locomotion.

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:37.120
Well, in our previous video, we have seen
how various parts of the body are

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:38.780
connected.

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:42.280
Now let’s have a look as to how animals
locomote, and how the

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:44.370
parts inside our body move!

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:49.210
Wait a second… do parts within us actually
move?

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:50.540
Of course they do!

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:53.879
Although we do not notice the organs inside
us bouncing,

00:00:53.879 --> 00:00:57.159
they all move in some or the other manner.

00:00:57.159 --> 00:01:01.760
Imagine if one fine day, the
stomach and intestines stop moving…

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:03.340
What would happen then?

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:06.760
Well, it
would be quite bad as the complete digestion

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:08.740
process would stop!

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:12.610
Similarly,
what if our lungs stop contraction and relaxation?

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:17.270
Well, needless to say, that
would cost us our life!

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:21.950
So then, who helps us with these important
processes of movement and

00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:22.950
locomotion?

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:23.950
Yes!

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:25.450
It’s the MUSCULAR tissue!

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:28.960
As the name says, muscular
means made up of muscles.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:33.450
The cells of this tissue are elongated and
contain

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:35.780
contractile proteins.

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:37.560
Now what could be these basically?

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:41.200
Well, when we say
movement, it means the muscles themselves

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:44.549
have to perform contraction and
relaxation right?

00:01:44.549 --> 00:01:48.609
So for this, the cells have proteins like
actin and myosin,

00:01:48.609 --> 00:01:54.429
which themselves contract or relax, thus enabling
the muscles to do the same.

00:01:54.429 --> 00:01:58.569
With this, does this mean that the tissue
will not have types?

00:01:58.569 --> 00:02:00.890
Because all
muscles are the same right?

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:04.179
Well, actually not!

00:02:04.179 --> 00:02:08.039
Muscles come in various types, depending on
their functions.

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:11.610
So are we trying
to say that muscles in our stomach are different

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:13.080
from those in our biceps?

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:14.790
Of
course yes!

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:19.330
The fact that both serve different functions
itself suggests that

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:23.099
their structure and nature is going to be
different.

00:02:23.099 --> 00:02:26.620
So what are the various types of Muscular
tissue?

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:31.200
Well, for simplicity of
understanding, we divide them into three major

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:32.709
categories.

00:02:32.709 --> 00:02:37.290
STRIATED Muscles,
SMOOTH muscles, and CARDIAC muscles.

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:39.980
Let’s have a look at each type.

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:42.299
The first one is called STRIATED muscles.

00:02:42.299 --> 00:02:48.290
Now as the name suggests, striated,
means when we stain this tissue and observe

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:53.099
it under the microscope, then it
exhibits striations in the form of alternate

00:02:53.099 --> 00:02:54.450
light and dark bands.

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:57.909
Where do we
find this type by the way?

00:02:57.909 --> 00:03:00.220
We find these striated muscles attached to
the

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:01.970
bones.

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:05.079
Hence they are also named as the SKELETAL
muscles.

00:03:05.079 --> 00:03:09.090
Also, when we
say attached to the skeleton, it means they

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:13.650
help in movement of our hands
and legs, along with locomotion.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:16.069
So can we say that their action is carried
out

00:03:16.069 --> 00:03:18.260
according to our will?

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:19.620
Absolutely!

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:24.290
And that is the reason we call them
Voluntary muscles also!

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:27.510
Thus, striated muscles are called as skeletal
muscles

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:30.819
and voluntary muscles as well.

00:03:30.819 --> 00:03:35.280
These are found to have a typical cylindrical
structure due to wrapping of the long muscle

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:37.349
fibres together.

00:03:37.349 --> 00:03:42.340
These are usually
unbranched and multi-nucleate in nature.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:47.269
On similar lines, we have the next type that
is ‘smooth muscles’.

00:03:47.269 --> 00:03:50.689
These are also
called as UNSTRIATED and INVOLUNTARY.

00:03:50.689 --> 00:03:56.500
Now we can guess their structure
and nature from the names itself!

00:03:56.500 --> 00:04:01.340
Smooth or unstriated because they do not
present a banding pattern like the striated

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:05.090
ones when stained and observed
under a microscope.

00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:10.650
Instead, they have long, spindle shaped, uni-nucleate
cells.

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:12.550
And why involuntary?

00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:13.550
Yes!

00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:15.769
Because their activities are not under our
control.

00:04:15.769 --> 00:04:19.060
So what could be their location now?

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:25.170
Well, we find smooth muscles in the
stomach, in the bronchi, in the uterus and

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:27.160
also in the iris of eyes.

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:31.900
Now let’s take a look at the last type that
is cardiac muscles.

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:37.510
Again, the name is
enough for us to state its function and location.

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:40.140
These muscles are those
found in the heart.

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:43.580
So the rhythmic contraction and relaxation
of our heart, or

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:48.820
the working of our heart, is all because of
these cardiac muscles.

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:52.260
Although they
are involuntary in activity, their structure

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:54.920
is like that of striated muscles
partially.

00:04:54.920 --> 00:05:00.530
They are cylindrical and branched, but uni-nucleate
in nature.

00:05:00.530 --> 00:05:03.750
These
muscles perform rhythmic contraction and relaxation

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:06.380
tirelessly throughout our
life!

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:11.930
Well, this was about the three major types
of muscular tissues – the striated,

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:14.290
smooth and cardiac!

00:05:14.290 --> 00:05:17.860
In the next video we will discuss nervous
tissues in

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:18.400
details.

